WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.382 --> 00:00:01.215
- What's up, Believe Nation?

00:00:01.215 --> 00:00:02.997
It's Evan, my one word is believe,

00:00:02.997 --> 00:00:06.122
and I believe in people more
than they believe in themselves

00:00:06.122 --> 00:00:08.429
and I want some of that
to rub off onto you

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:11.029
so you can go off and
create those amazing things

00:00:11.029 --> 00:00:13.296
that you've got up in here,
bring them out to the world.

00:00:13.296 --> 00:00:15.317
So, to you help you in your
journey, today we're going to look

00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:18.615
at the seven habits of
self-made millionaires.

00:00:18.615 --> 00:00:21.144
Habit #7 is my personal
favorite, and I'd love to know

00:00:21.144 --> 00:00:22.742
which one you guys like the best,

00:00:22.742 --> 00:00:24.261
and, as always, as you're watching,

00:00:24.261 --> 00:00:25.758
if you hear something
that really resonates

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:28.430
with you, leave it down
in the comments below

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.127
because, when you write it down,

00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:32.491
it's must more likely
to stick with yourself

00:00:32.491 --> 00:00:34.589
as well as inspire other people.

00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:35.550
Enjoy.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:36.557
(jet flying)

00:00:36.557 --> 00:00:38.974
(sonic boom)

00:00:40.209 --> 00:00:42.959
(ethereal music)

00:00:44.495 --> 00:00:47.734
- I should mention one
thing about reading.

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:49.887
It was at the library here at Columbia--

00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:50.720
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:51.667
- that I wish I'd spent probably more

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.845
time than in any other student.

00:00:54.845 --> 00:00:57.602
I lived there, practically.

00:00:57.602 --> 00:00:59.620
And I pulled a book out there,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.971
happen to be "Who's Who in America"

00:01:00.971 --> 00:01:02.858
and that told me something
about my professor,

00:01:02.858 --> 00:01:05.438
Benjamin Graham, and then
I went to the library,

00:01:05.438 --> 00:01:07.373
and I said I want to learn more about this

00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:09.372
because I learned this over here.

00:01:09.372 --> 00:01:10.718
That changed my whole life.

00:01:10.718 --> 00:01:13.064
You know, we own Geico now (chuckles)

00:01:13.064 --> 00:01:16.486
because of that librarian directing me

00:01:16.486 --> 00:01:19.366
to some other book, and then
following through on that.

00:01:19.366 --> 00:01:20.783
It's the chance--

00:01:22.286 --> 00:01:25.538
I read about 1/5th the
pace that Bill does,

00:01:25.538 --> 00:01:28.087
but I still spend five or
six hours a day reading.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.523
I mean, you can learn so much.

00:01:29.523 --> 00:01:30.997
I particularly love biography,

00:01:30.997 --> 00:01:35.164
just to be able to live the
lives of these people that have

00:01:36.238 --> 00:01:41.008
been so extraordinary, the
lessons, and the discouragements

00:01:41.008 --> 00:01:42.775
they faced, just everything about 'em,

00:01:42.775 --> 00:01:46.113
so you can't get enough of reading.

00:01:46.113 --> 00:01:48.863
(ethereal music)

00:01:51.062 --> 00:01:54.711
- I have to show up at work
with the right attitude

00:01:54.711 --> 00:01:57.294
and with the positive energy.

00:01:57.294 --> 00:02:00.961
And, I actually had a
t-shirt made that said

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.228
"positive energy is a part
of your job description."

00:02:06.228 --> 00:02:07.061
Right?

00:02:07.061 --> 00:02:11.560
So, the idea that I want to
show up with the right energy,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.803
I want to show up, we're all
here, even now we're all here,

00:02:15.803 --> 00:02:17.467
we're trying to feed our families,

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.821
we're trying to have a good time,

00:02:19.821 --> 00:02:21.179
we're trying to--

00:02:21.179 --> 00:02:23.982
this is an important part of our lives,

00:02:23.982 --> 00:02:26.099
our time is all that we have.

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:28.474
So, to me, it's hugely important

00:02:28.474 --> 00:02:32.641
to deliver positive energy
in a way because it's viral.

00:02:33.539 --> 00:02:36.950
So, if I come with positive energy,

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.484
then someone else is going to
pick up on the positive energy,

00:02:39.484 --> 00:02:41.892
and you're going to take
it home to your families.

00:02:41.892 --> 00:02:44.279
So, for me, it's just hugely important

00:02:44.279 --> 00:02:47.930
to approach everything and
everybody at every turn

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.097
with the most positive, loving
kindness that I can generate.

00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:57.735
(ethereal music)

00:02:59.947 --> 00:03:01.492
- I get up at crazy hours, whether

00:03:01.492 --> 00:03:02.359
it's three o'clock in the morning--

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:03.865
By the way, when we
were talking in London,

00:03:03.865 --> 00:03:05.371
I was getting up at three, 'cause I had

00:03:05.371 --> 00:03:08.327
to be on set by four, I'm sorry, by seven.

00:03:08.327 --> 00:03:11.631
So, whatever time my call
time is, and I tell this

00:03:11.631 --> 00:03:15.325
to studios and directors,
and our producers.

00:03:15.325 --> 00:03:19.405
So, my call time is at seven,
then you back your clock up

00:03:19.405 --> 00:03:22.491
four hours, and then that's
when I get up and train twice.

00:03:22.491 --> 00:03:23.670
I'll get my cardio in and breakfast

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.814
and then I'll go hit the weights.

00:03:25.814 --> 00:03:29.993
Clangin' and bangin', we call
it, jackin' iron. (chuckles)

00:03:29.993 --> 00:03:31.643
- But, what time do you go to bed,

00:03:31.643 --> 00:03:34.441
I mean you might shoot
really late at night.

00:03:34.441 --> 00:03:35.551
- On a movie I'll average probably

00:03:35.551 --> 00:03:37.718
about five hours of sleep.

00:03:39.047 --> 00:03:41.797
(ethereal music)

00:03:43.397 --> 00:03:44.230
(audience laughter)

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.670
- Do you remember the audition
when you first met De Niro?

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:50.025
- That was one of those
incredible auditions

00:03:50.025 --> 00:03:51.841
that came around that, I suppose,

00:03:51.841 --> 00:03:54.955
every kid had been waiting
for for a long period of time.

00:03:54.955 --> 00:03:57.474
Because, it's just not that often

00:03:57.474 --> 00:04:01.059
that you get a starring
role above Robert De Niro,

00:04:01.059 --> 00:04:04.062
and this incredible screenplay.

00:04:04.062 --> 00:04:06.156
It was Ellen Barkin and
Michael Caton-Jones,

00:04:06.156 --> 00:04:10.323
and it was just, everyone
was fiending for this role.

00:04:11.162 --> 00:04:13.052
I got down, I suppose to the final five,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.885
and it was a last day.

00:04:16.172 --> 00:04:21.059
Toby was there and I do
remember thinking to myself,

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:23.892
I got to do something to stand out.

00:04:25.713 --> 00:04:28.285
In the audition, there was Art
Linson, Michael Caton-Jones,

00:04:28.285 --> 00:04:31.873
Art Linson was the producer, and De Niro.

00:04:31.873 --> 00:04:35.456
And, I remember it was
a mustard jar scene.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.078
And, he had to jam a mustard
jar in my eye repeatedly.

00:04:40.078 --> 00:04:42.696
It was an abuse scene.

00:04:42.696 --> 00:04:45.748
The script didn't call
for it, but I got up.

00:04:45.748 --> 00:04:47.348
He said, "Now is it empty, is it empty?"

00:04:47.348 --> 00:04:50.644
And I got up and I screamed no!

00:04:50.644 --> 00:04:53.450
Completely unnecessary,
but I screamed in his face.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:54.463
(audience laughs)

00:04:54.463 --> 00:04:56.489
And I sat there (chuckles), you know,

00:04:56.489 --> 00:04:59.337
with my head lookin' like a red tomato

00:04:59.337 --> 00:05:03.985
and everyone started laughing
at me in the entire room.

00:05:03.985 --> 00:05:06.222
De Niro, everybody was in hysterics

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:07.480
and I sat there frozen thinking

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.276
oh, my God, I just screwed
this entire opportunity up.

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:12.401
(audience laughs)

00:05:12.401 --> 00:05:14.847
And I just sat there frozen, and then Bob,

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:18.095
in traditional De Niro fashion
just looked at me and went,

00:05:18.095 --> 00:05:20.007
"It's good, it's good."

00:05:20.007 --> 00:05:22.840
(audience laughs)

00:05:26.857 --> 00:05:31.255
And then, we carried on doing
this scene, and I actually

00:05:31.255 --> 00:05:33.987
had dinner with De Niro
recently, and he told me,

00:05:33.987 --> 00:05:37.987
"I was the one who said
you should get the job."

00:05:39.823 --> 00:05:42.573
(ethereal music)

00:05:44.596 --> 00:05:46.420
